#{[17592186382625 #edb/ref 1080 "-1+5\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382625 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382625 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382626 #edb/ref 1080 "5-2\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382626 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382626 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14945f42-7413-59f1-969e-cad3bb3baa75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f249579b-a99f-5b51-b0c7-e7f72d9a4512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389442 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389443 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3b3b8e1e-c87a-5182-99ea-c5567dd2e35b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389444 #edb/ref 1080 "253\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389444 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389444 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56559d07-36b9-5fba-b51e-1f8e5cba51e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389445 #edb/ref 1080 "275\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389445 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389445 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c613f699-7516-5c10-a37c-3efc4c225fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389446 #edb/ref 1080 "242\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389446 #edb/ref 108